I wouldn’t automatically defer to a more intelligent species. Intelligence and moral wisdom are not the same: a highly capable civilization could still be indifferent, exploitative, or mistaken.

I would be open to learning from them if there were strong evidence that:

- their intentions were peaceful and cooperative;
- their knowledge was reliable and independently verifiable;
- interacting with them would not endanger humanity or remove human autonomy;
- their guidance was consistent with broadly defensible values such as reducing suffering and preserving the ability to choose our future.

In that case, I’d treat them as potentially valuable teachers—not as unquestionable authorities. “More intelligent” might justify attention and humility, but not unconditional obedience. The goal should remain helping humanity make informed, safe choices, including learning from superior beings when doing so genuinely benefits humanity.
